<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.8.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01864</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.8.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4d9bb574-5d64-4b97-b5e5-f3b679885037</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para><emphasis><emphasis>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Choose the expression that best describes the diagram.  </item>
</list>
    
    <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a4c39cadbd12167dc25e8a8977547823585c8403"/></media></figure><list>
    <item>\((2x+5)(x+3)\) is equivalent to \(2x^2+5x+8\). </item>
    <item>\((2x +5)(x +3)\) is equivalent to \(2x^2+14x + 23\). </item>
    <item>\((2x+5)(x+3)\) is equivalent to \(2x^2+11x+15\). </item>
    <item>\((2x +5)(x +3)\) is equivalent to \(30x^2\). </item>
  </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\((2x+5)(x+3)\) is equivalent to \(2x^2+11x+15\). </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>In questions a–d, match each expanded quadratic expression with an equivalent expression written as a product.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item>\(x^2+12x+32\)
      <list>
        <item>\((x+2)(x+6)\) </item>
        <item> \((x+4)(x+2)\)</item>
        <item> \((x+8)(x+4)\)</item>
        <item>\((x+2)(x+3)\)</item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<emphasis> </emphasis>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\((x+8)(x+4)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> \(x^2+5x+6\)
      <list>
        <item> \((x+2)(x+6)\) </item>
        <item> \((x+4)(x+2)\) </item>
        <item> \((x+8)(x+4)\) </item>
        <item> \((x+2)(x+3)\) </item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((x+2)(x+3)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item>\(x^2+6x+8\)
      <list>
        <item> \((x+2)(x+6)\) </item>
        <item> \((x+4)(x+2)\) </item>
        <item> \((x+8)(x+4)\) </item>
        <item> \((x+2)(x+3)\) </item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((x+4)(x+2)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
    <item>\(x^2+8x+12\)
      <list>
        <item>\( (x+2)(x+6)\)</item>
        <item> \((x+4)(x+2)\)</item>
        <item> \((x+8)(x+4)\)</item>
        <item> \((x+2)(x+3)\)</item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((x+2)(x+6)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> expressions that are equivalent to \(x^2+4x\).  </item>
</list>
    <list>
      <item> \(x(x+4)\) </item>
      <item> \((x+2)^2\) </item>
      <item> \((x+x)(x+4)\) </item>
      <item>\((x+2)^2-4\) </item>
      <item>\((x+4)x\) </item>
    </list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x(x+4)\), \((x+2)^2-4\), \((x+4)x\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Tyler drew a diagram to expand \((x+5)(2x+3)\).</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="Rectangle partitioned into 4 smaller rectangles. The top is labeled 2 x and 3. The side is labeled x and 5. Inside, the top row contains 2 x squared and 3 x. The bottom row contains 7 x and 8."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3257ad167a891e11df938a70502ad9c0fd909998"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> Tyler’s diagram is incorrect. Choose the sentence that describes Tyler’s mistake.
      <list>
        <item> Tyler added 5 and 2 and 5 and 3 instead of multiplying them.  </item>
        <item> Tyler multiplied 5 and 2 and 5 and 3 instead of adding them.  </item>
        <item> Tyler should have labeled the top \(2x\) and 5 and the side \(x\) and 3. </item>
        <item> Tyler should have labeled the top \(x\) and 3 and the side \(2x\) and 5. </item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Tyler added 5 and 2 and 5 and 3 instead of multiplying them. </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> What is the correct expanded form of \((x+5)(2x+3)\)?
      <list>
        <item>\(3x+8\)</item>
        <item>\( 30x^2\) </item>
        <item> \(2x^2+10x+8\) </item>
        <item> \(2x^2+13x+15\) </item>
      </list>
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(2x^2+13x+15\)</para></section>
</content>
